Hey Luke, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:21,280
Excited to be here.

13
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,280
I wanted to kick things off with how you think about growth.

14
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:37,200
You're at eleven labs, you guys are launching tonnes of cool stuff, working on amazing products, but you've also been in the industry a while and have been doing this for a long time, working on growth.

15
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:39,840
So how do you think at a high level about growth?

16
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,880
Yeah, with growth, we think about building growth.

17
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:58,240
engines and what a growth engine for us is it's a system which we track and we optimize and we set ambitious goals and we repeatedly take this engineering approach to marketing where we try and level up every time we do it.

18
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:12,480
And so one specific way we do this is through big launches and something which is very unique about AI is that when you get a new model that unlock a new capability which gives you a new viral moment.

19
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:30,720
And so the heartbeat of the growth team is working hand in hand with our incredible product and research team, so that when they're launching new features, new models, new capabilities, we put it through this growth system and that normally translates into what is hopefully a really viral moment.

20
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:34,800
So for example, we have the world's best speech to text model.

21
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:44,560
And so when we release Scribe v two, it's like, okay, let's do an incredible video and tweet thread to really show the world how to do that and topple the benchmarks.

22
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,200
But then that also translates behind the scenes into a world class landing page.

23
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:53,840
It's also time to updatedate all your different meta, your LinkedIn ads, your Google ads.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,320
So yeah, the launches are really the heartbeat for us.

25
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:56,720
Yeah.

26
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:59,680
I think this is really overlooked in the broader industry.

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:15,200
And when you're working on a feature, ideally you're working on something that people are going to care about and that wouldn't be just your users, like it's a great opportunity to like expand that messaging beyond just like putting it in the app and putting it in that banner.

28
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:36,640
Although some people don't even do that, you know, a lot of apps don't even have a like on launch open, what's new and hey, check out this new feature or a tooltip or, you know, those kind of of things to even hint at existing users that this new capability is there, much less using all that engineering effort and the creativity that goes into this new feature as a moment to get more attention.

29
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:48,640
And, you know, of course, people are going to be listening like, Oh, well, look, earned media is so hard to get these days, and it is, and Eleven Labs has an easier time than a lot will have getting that earned media, but you don't know until you try.

30
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:50,560
And I think more apps should be trying.

31
00:03:51,040 --> 00:04:06,960
And then how do you think about taking those launch moments and using the ideas, the new features and things like that in your paid assets as well, because it's not just about getting earned media, it's about using these new features and that creativity to get attention, even if it's paid attention.

32
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:08,160
Yeah, one hundred percent.

33
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:16,800
So whenever we do a launch, part of the big bit is, okay, how does this translate into our meta ads, into our TikTok ads?

34
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:23,760
And so we have an eleven labs app which has all the best creative models, whether it's speech, sound effects, music.

35
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:28,400
And as part of that, we release, for example, the image and video feature within it.

36
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:34,560
As part of that, you then go, okay, what are the ads that we should do, what are the different hooks?

37
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:41,920
And as you're thinking and iterating on that core messaging for the ex post, you're also thinking and iterating for the different ag copy.

38
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:56,000
And one of the new things we've actually just built, which has been really cool, is we've built a custom GPT where we've analyzed what are all the top performing metro and Google copy and what's all our bottom performing metro and Google copy.

39
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:06,640
So we normally take what's our like brand ex post and then we pass it through this custom GPT which will then iterate on it into like the high performing ag.

40
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:19,360
And then another big bit, which I'd be happy to dive into, is also we want to create lots of different variants of images of videos of statics in order to see what will drive conversion.

41
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:32,000
Yeah, I did want to dive more specifically into video, I mean, you know, between reels and TikTok and I mean, even a good ex post with a video is going to generally perform better than just like a thread without any kind of example.

42
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:40,640
How do you think about generating these assets, when to use AI creative, when to lean into UGC and when to just team create?

43
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:41,280
assets.

44
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:46,880
On your blog post, you talk about like putting the founder in front of a camera and loom, uh, showing off the product.

45
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:49,120
People don't often do that kind of thing.

46
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:54,880
But if again, if you're building something worth attention, you can probably make a little video like that.

47
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,200
So how do you how do you think about balancing all this?

48
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:58,480
Yeah, one hundred percent.

49
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,680
So I think this will change a lot in the next year or two.

50
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:08,480
But now in Feb 2026, what we found best is normally actually you start with agencies.

51
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:10,000
So we started with agencies.

52
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:11,840
You're testing lots of different formats.

53
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,040
You work out what fits.

54
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:14,480
But then you want to bring it in.

55
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:18,640
house, like really optimize the quality but also scale up the quantity.

56
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:28,480
And so we have two people which have the job title AI creative producers and their task is every day they're just obsessed.

57
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:29,840
They're using tools like motion.

58
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,120
They're obsessed with, okay, what's the data, what's working so far?

59
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:35,360
What are other companies in the space doing?

60
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:40,160
And then they're coming up with new creative ideas and then rapidly iterating and testing it.

61
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:48,640
At the moment, what we found works really well for our core apps, our web app is they create with After Effects really great motion design.

62
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:50,160
So that's their background.

63
00:06:50,240 --> 00:07:10,800
They are motion designers, but then they layer in with text or speech voiceovers using Leavenlabs with the music, and that allows you to do like rapid testing because you can test different hooks with motion design, you can test different voiceovers, different music, so you get nice little variants, but you also are able to keep the quality bar really high.

64
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:19,280
And other things we use to boost production is a lot of the AI generated images we also incorporate into the motion design.

65
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,600
Are you still using UTC though?

66
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:33,600
And like, how do you think about about UGC in this mix of AI generated creative or AI assisted creative plus, you know, the in-house assets and you have motion designers on staff, like you've got a lot going on.

67
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,200
Are you also working with UGC?

68
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,600
Yeah, we do three main types of video production in-house.

69
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:44,960
So the first one is the motion design and that's still the bulk of our ads and works like really well.

70
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:53,280
You can get like the quick hooks and variants to really see like what messaging resonates and particularly with this like AI abstract UI based flows.

71
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:55,040
They're like great ways to show it.

72
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:01,520
The other team we have is a motion design team but focused on on brand videos and launches.

73
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,120
And so they spend like longer amount of time, fewer variants.

74
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:16,800
And then we also have an in-house creator called Alec who has a YouTube channel who just shows like how you get the very best out of these tools and it's more like a person in front of the camera.

75
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:23,200
I think there's definitely a world where we can like try supercharging that in the future for the educational side using the avatars.

76
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:33,520
But for now, we really care about like quality as well as quantity and like actually a person in the studio making really fantastic vide educational videos is a great way to do that.

77
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:33,920
Yeah.

78
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:42,240
You shared with me before the podcast that you're on track to spend over $100 million in paid ads by the end of the year.

